# What a generated project runs on macOS and Linux, as SHA-256 over each file's bytes, written by
# scripts/fence.py. A line changes only with a reason appended below it, saying why what the file does on
# macOS and Linux is unchanged (docs/windows-plan.md, D2). Each reason carries the digest of the record it
# vouches for; editing a line by hand breaks that.
#
# why: 2026-11-09 7406116f1e78db81 The fence is put up over today's tree, before any change for Windows.
# why: 2026-11-09 001fff112e2f6aa5 24.4: models.py and benchmark.py start their sibling scripts with PYTHON, which is "python3" wherever sys.platform is not win32 — the argv macOS and Linux ran before.
# why: 2026-11-09 a89a3c4b51ee4793 24.5: init passes --script sh to Spec Kit only where uname -s says MINGW, MSYS or CYGWIN; on Darwin and Linux the case matches nothing and specify gets the arguments it had.
# why: 2026-11-09 628ad48c4fc19d99 24.8: try_it ends an app's group through end_tree, os.killpg with the same signals off Windows; moorings links through link_folder and is_link, os.symlink and is_symlink off Windows (isjunction is False there); the harbourmaster's symlink_to line is unchanged off Windows.
# why: 2026-11-09 16780b2335a643ce 24.11: the event model's npm install runs as 'cd scripts/event-model && npm install' instead of 'npm --prefix scripts/event-model install', which npm on Windows ignores; each recipe line is its own shell, so the same tree is installed in the same place on macOS and Linux.
# why: 2026-11-09 d2a8de915041746c 24.3: the root Makefile sets .SHELLFLAGS := -hc inside ifeq ($(OS),Windows_NT); OS is unset on macOS and Linux, so the block is skipped and SHELL := /bin/bash runs every line as before.
# why: 2026-11-09 5b5c6f2aa0d51bd8 Off Windows: moorings.leave removes a junction only on os.name nt, and the harbourmaster's refused-rebase message adds git's own last line where it named no file; neither changes what runs on macOS or Linux.
# why: 2026-11-10 7d4301fb5aae4164 Not Windows work, and deliberately a change on every platform: check-slice-scope lets a slice write specs/<feature>/people/ (sail.md rung 12 already told it to) and, on the event profile, the chart check-chart holds to the model, as it lets model.drawio through; logs.py refuses a fleet-ticked need with no check on write and reads one already written as a person's, as fleet.py tell already refused it.
# why: 2026-11-10 f81a3ff6cd1ddce5 Not Windows work, and deliberately a change on every platform: under /cruise the captain resumes a parked slice as answered by decisions only when its newest park was waiting on questions undecided when it was written, the rule the harbourmaster already clears by; a park written after the decisions no longer re-dispatches the slice every turn.
# why: 2026-11-10 3136b0a202ff0d6f harbourmaster: a remote with no trunk yet is given trunk by the first merge, instead of every merge staying local; a remote that has trunk, and a harbour with none, are unchanged
# why: 2026-11-10 d1f1f2c4da4a92cc moorings.berth tries git worktree add again only when git refused over a sibling's half-made .git/worktrees entry; a successful add, and every other refusal, does exactly what it did
# why: 2026-11-10 e257e81f23812fcf check-python and the fleet preflight now refuse Python 3.10 up front; the toolkit already imports 3.11-only names, so 3.10 failed later anyway, and 3.11 or newer behaves exactly as before
# why: 2026-11-11 76707b774934bb56 acp.py opens an agent's pipes as UTF-8 by name; macOS and Linux read them as UTF-8 already, and a byte that is not UTF-8 is now replaced where it ended the reader
# why: 2026-11-11 ea37558164d2a25f the stand-in agent offers a terminal sign-in only to a client that says it can run one, and refuses a prompt as signed out
# why: 2026-11-11 bad785543f7a5824 the stand-in agent offers a terminal sign-in only to a client that says it can run one, and refuses a prompt as signed out
# why: 2026-11-11 f066e7f867af051c the board's protocol client says it can have a terminal sign-in run, and a fault carries the agent's error code
# why: 2026-11-11 b3631ca06a7c7970 the merge gate's scratch worktree carries the skills extensions install as well as their tools, and a cleared park under a stopped line is carried on mid-turn
# why: 2026-11-11 b19e297951d0d356 the registry names the npm package and the command of each harness's own CLI
# why: 2026-11-12 5a5aaa986729f7a7 main's registry change and this branch's merge-gate fix meet in one merge
# why: 2026-11-13 3b8a61de97e2d746 phase 25.4 (docs/stop-the-line-plan.md), owner's rule 2026-11-13: under /cruise with a deciding `decide`, a late told and the decision ceiling no longer force a boundary. A deliberate behaviour change on every platform, cruise only; outside cruise inbox.py and captain.py behave as before
# why: 2026-11-13 747d8788e453b0ee phase 25.2/25.3 (docs/stop-the-line-plan.md), owner's rule 2026-11-13: a red trunk stops the line and the harbourmaster sends the trunk mender (new scripts/agents/mender.py, harbour lines mend/mended/mend-failed); captains hold on red; broke_it reads the grant. A deliberate behaviour change on every platform; 'mender: off' in harbour.json keeps a red parking for a person
# why: 2026-11-13 d3d6851285dabce5 logs.py: comments only, shortened to its size budget; behaviour unchanged
# why: 2026-11-13 3d2c262c657bc8bb Phase 25.1, the owner's decision of 2026-11-13: every toolkit script reads and writes the run's own state under .slipwai/run/ through logs.run_dir, and the ignore block has one line for it. This is a deliberate path change, not a behaviour-preserving edit: a project whose .gitignore lacks .slipwai/run/ keeps the old places, and slipwai migrate moves the state in.
# why: 2026-11-13 c563d5c8c98b73aa mender.py: the record's dict is built on its own line so the write carries its encoding on one line; behaviour unchanged
# why: 2026-11-13 b4743bd7886552d3 mender.py: a zombie attempt reads as ended where /proc says so, and SLIPWAI_MENDER is split on whitespace like SLIPWAI_SAIL so a Windows path keeps its backslashes; macOS and Linux behave as before for a live or ended attempt
# why: 2026-11-13 305759175e9fb0bf fleet.py: start, relight and tell refuse an app (SLIPWAI_APP_VERSION) older than project.json's generator.updatedWith, since it would write where the project no longer reads; with no SLIPWAI_APP_VERSION, as from a terminal, nothing changes
# why: 2026-11-13 072a7386369e894f render-fairways.py: --check no longer compares the split's held_by, which is the moving claim refs; the fold still writes it. The Makefile's check-rendered help line says so. A deliberate behaviour change on every platform, owner's word 2026-11-13
# why: 2026-11-13 6b5ad224680ec5ce captain.py: a park on overdue messages names who wrote them; travel.py: a wip push retries its lease on the forge's value when it is a commit this branch held, and names another machine only for one it never held; check-crossing.py: core.hooksPath naming this checkout's own .githooks in full is respelled .githooks. Deliberate fixes on every platform
# why: 2026-11-16 9a91006649d6d2a4 a-run-stops-waiting-on-a-person: scripts/agents/deck_line.py is new; captain, harbourmaster, mender, harness, drive, session and try_it changed for the gate, the green-trunk rule, the limit words, the mender's branches and a story's dates — nothing platform-specific
# why: 2026-11-16 875c597002bc1e66 a-run-stops-waiting-on-a-person: the captain's prompt names /sail, the command, rather than /drive
# why: 2026-11-16 3c9ae80eb41713a6 a-run-stops-waiting-on-a-person, second part: the harbourmaster sees stopping captains off and carries a rebase past append-only conflicts, a berth's log link is held to where it goes, and a captain puts a need in its fairway's own code to its session — nothing platform-specific beyond the stop grace, which Windows never reaches
# why: 2026-11-16 809e032cb46b6207 try_it.py gains the film verb (start, chapter, stop): one recording per capability with its marks; no existing verb changes
# why: 2026-11-16 bbcd89f3d39e50fd 2026-11-16, changelog.d/demo-is-feedback.md: cruise.py takes a demos setting (hand by default, or person); deck_line.py writes a capability's accepted and demo-due lines; clearance.py and capabilities.py read demos to say whether a capability waits on a person. Deliberate, and the same on macOS and Linux; nothing else these files do moved
# why: 2026-11-16 0f477ca6b75219fd 2026-11-16, changelog.d/demo-is-feedback.md (review): deck_line.py refuses an accepted or demo-due unless the capability is whole and cleared, takes only the hand's acceptance and refuses the line the demos setting does not take; capabilities.py has the one rule (acceptable), a --json port, says under hand that nothing is owed a person, and reads the hand's acceptance off a register or demo-log written before the line existed. Deliberate, and the same on macOS and Linux; nothing else these files do moved
# why: 2026-11-16 82c619b40e11079c merge of the held driver and the demo acceptance: drive.py, captain.py, talk.py, abreast.py and cruise.py (sessions: auto) move with the driver held open and the conversation files; deck_line.py and capabilities.py with the hand's acceptance; no other behaviour on macOS and Linux moves
# why: 2026-11-16 dd6dcf4c3d4a4a5f 2026-11-16, changelog.d/driver-held-open.md second review: talk.py signs every message for its slice and makes its key private; drive.py verifies a pid before ending it, falls back to print where auto cannot take the protocol, and reads absent sessions as print; captain.py bounds a captain's turn by deck lines and commits only and a message's by time, and lets only a person lift a person's hold; cruise.py reads absent sessions as print. Print mode and every project that does not say auto run on macOS and Linux as before
# why: 2026-11-16 926e2472f0141a76 merge of the decision log: check-decisions.py asks a new decision for one plain sentence and spares entries written before the rule and slices then in flight; no other behaviour on macOS and Linux moves
# why: 2026-11-16 c9a3ecf5f8c2489b 2026-11-16: merging captain-agent (evals, decision log) into stream E: the record re-taken over both sides; nothing either changed beyond what each recorded
# why: 2026-11-16 b160bf7ec571a9d8 2026-11-16, stream E review: the captain agent's orders are split into captain_orders.py and captain_guard.py; a routed message is the captain's own with the person quoted; a captain's answers are deck decisions; attempts are read from the log; a headless captain runs in its own folder and is checked; a word to a slice answers only the park about it. With no captain agent a stream on macOS and Linux runs as before, but for a slice-aimed word clearing only the park about that slice.
# why: 2026-11-16 ab728d565ecc7c2b talk.py's budget line re-claimed for the quote field; nothing it does changed
# why: 2026-11-16 a48320ed1d292cb7 2026-11-16, stream E second review: the captain agent runs only over the protocol (captain_guard.py removed, captain_agent.py's process path kept for the evals alone); a slice's runs are bounded at the dispatch by its dispatched deck lines; a routed message is only of a genuine one and names whose words it quotes; captain.answers is off by default. With no captain agent a stream on macOS and Linux runs as before except that a slice parks for a person after 2 x attempts + 1 runs since a person last spoke to it.
# why: 2026-11-16 80a492f1150cfe11 2026-11-16: whose park a word answers is one rule, logs.about, shared by the captain, the harbourmaster and the lighting check: a park names a slice by its field or by its whole id at the head of its why. Otherwise unchanged on macOS and Linux.
# why: 2026-11-16 681e847b44ed1dae the captain's turn is not over while a choice it put to its agent is still open; nothing changes without an agent
# why: 2026-11-16 095de8408e35b11b 2026-11-16, stream E fourth review: a choice put to the captain is always answered and the turn can end; a person's pause is written as it is heard and nobody else's stop or pause replaces it; a malformed order or message is refused, never raised on; a person's words are routed once, by their own event, quoted apart; only a person's proved word resets a slice's runs; the captain session runs orders.py only under this interpreter and reads no key. Without a captain agent the one change on macOS and Linux is the pausing line written when a person's pause is heard.
# why: 2026-11-16 59b20d9ecf4d17d9 2026-11-16, stream E fifth review: one reader for every JSON file taken from the run folder (plain files only, bounded, never raising) and one boundary that turns any failure of the captain agent's machinery into the program alone; a person's pausing line is itself the hold, and only a person's resume, always written, lifts it; any person's told gives a slice its runs back; the captain is handed its exact order command. Without an agent, on macOS and Linux, a person's told gives runs back again as before 1d04f733, and a file the reader refuses no longer stops the captain.
# why: 2026-11-16 58f6ebc432207ae8 the captain program's word that its agent failed carries its own read receipt, so a stream never parks waiting on it
# why: 2026-11-17 3fe1fb6207d9ad64 the captain agent's sixth review: a stop is not an Exception, one reader for the run folder, a noted deck line nothing waits on, one path that lets the agent go, and the order command in the prompt alone
# why: 2026-11-17 85b6c1cdd264aa95 the captain agent's sixth review: a stop is not an Exception, one reader for the run folder, a noted deck line nothing waits on, one path that lets the agent go, and the order command in the prompt alone
# why: 2026-11-17 047fda413f134276 captain.py: a noted line is no slice's progress in mine(), and a captain's park is written with cause other, which the deck log's closed set has; talk.py: the thread is read to the end its first read found, and hold() says since when a slice is held. Behaviour on macOS and Linux is otherwise unchanged
# why: 2026-11-17 7bde268ef766aca8 whole-branch review of the captain agent: the captain session's policy parses an order command as a shell would, a captain's ask, pause, park needs and results do what they say, the evals' door in orders.py needs their marker, and words the captain acted on are read; a project with no captain agent behaves on macOS and Linux as before, apart from one line said where SLIPWAI_CAPTAIN in the environment overrides harbour.json
# why: 2026-11-17 c96ab4d578f6f95e whole-branch review, round 2: what a captain reads and lets a slice read is decided on the resolved path, its session's policy judges the whole request (a client keeps each tool call's updates for the permission asked of it), only words answer a person's words, a held slice not yet started is said with its holder, and an environment override is said off the deck; a project with no captain agent runs on macOS and Linux exactly as before
# why: 2026-11-17 74d6c3e01c3eb587 scripts/agents/surfaces.py refuses, with exit 1 and a sentence, a drawing whose research.md does not declare Blockers open: N and Questions open: N (changelog.d/stage-talk.md); a drawing that declares them, one ended at its budget, and every other path behave as before on macOS and Linux
# why: 2026-11-17 d5d4c898e53a1c51 scripts/agents/surfaces.py no longer fails a drawing whose research.md lacks the two counts: it reads them forgivingly (CRLF, bold, list markers, case) and prints a note, exiting as it did before the counts existed; every other path behaves as before on macOS and Linux
# why: 2026-11-17 7195a540f5479f1b a stage that had already ended when its captain ended it is ended, not a PermissionError the slice parks on
# why: 2026-11-17 092aea0b14d6c3ca scripts/agents/surfaces.py reads the two counts only where a line begins with the words and says a whole number, and still only prints a note; every other path behaves as before on macOS and Linux
# why: 2026-11-17 a5546cfb62f5cf44 an agent launched through the package runner is launched one at a time on a machine and tried once more where the runner fell over
# why: 2026-11-17 c4268550b7e4ca4a mender.py: docstring only. check-dispatch.py: also reads MTG-01-style slice ids where a project's plans name them so, and says which plan files it read when they name none; pure Python string handling, identical on macOS and Linux, no platform branch touched
# why: 2026-11-17 7a59fcc456a1c4f1 Not Windows work, and deliberately a change on every platform: check-slice-scope lets a slice add its own flag's declaration to infra/service/flags.auto.tfvars, seeded off and additively, since check-flags passes the declaration only in the same change as its reader; the realm files gain a public web client the password grant may use, so a story that signs in can seed.
# why: 2026-11-17 38e17315f6867180 Not Windows work, and deliberately a change on every platform: check-slice-scope reads the product's state through scripts/agents/release_state.py, a new standard-library module carried into every project, and under pilot holds a capability's entry to its last slice; a project with no state recorded merges flagged as before.
# why: 2026-11-17 68b004cd9fe48155 Not Windows work, and the same on every platform: the backing-service pruner asks git which of an option's matching paths it ignores, and counts none of those as the option installed; git check-ignore runs the same everywhere, and a path outside a repository is counted as before.
# why: 2026-11-18 e4c300a544886a35 Windows work for the captain-agent stream, macOS and Linux unchanged: the captain's named command is split as drive.words already split a slice's (backslashes kept only on Windows); a print-mode program is looked up on PATH only on Windows, where an npm .cmd shim is not found by a bare name; drive.alive asks the process handle only on Windows, where os.kill(pid, 0) is a Ctrl-C; drive.end_group and the captain's timed-out command end the tree with taskkill /T only on Windows and keep killpg elsewhere; captain_orders.unreadable compares against os.sep, which is / on macOS and Linux; check-dispatch prints its project paths with as_posix, the same text off Windows; the pruner's check-ignore is NUL-separated, the same answer off Windows.
# why: 2026-11-18 df30366479d37207 Windows work, macOS and Linux unchanged: talk.send stamps each message file's mtime with an instant later than the last this process sent, which off Windows is the clock's own, so the order fold draws is the same; drive.end_group's Windows branch always passes taskkill /F, and the POSIX branch is unchanged.
# why: 2026-11-18 75b2fb6d165c2ced Windows work, macOS and Linux unchanged: talk.instant spaces two stamps of one process a microsecond apart instead of a nanosecond, because NTFS keeps file times in 100 ns units; off Windows a stamp is still the clock's own, and the order fold draws is the same.
# why: 2026-11-18 2463382b32e22043 Same on every platform: release_state.move writes state after shape where there is one, the line metadata writes it on, so a migration merges it rather than keeping it twice; nothing it reads changes.
# why: 2026-11-18 87deb35d58df59e6 check-migration.py --confirm passes once no slice on the chart is unplaced, and says rather than refuses a listed name the chart lacks; check-model's two naming rules pass the event names .specify/event-names-from.json lists, read by check.py and the new held-names.ts. No other behaviour moves on macOS or Linux
# why: 2026-11-18 5ee271d0234d973a Same on every platform: the harbourmaster's spent_today adds the work tokens of stages that ended today, read from each slice's benchmark.json on trunk and in every berth by scripts/agents/spend.py, a new standard-library module that finds the berths in git's own worktree files and starts no process; nothing it starts, signals or writes changes.
# why: 2026-11-18 e36ed1264e72144b talk.py draws a message id of eight random bytes instead of three, and again where a waiting or handled message already has it. Behaviour changes on purpose and the same on every platform: two messages of one second no longer share a file
# why: 2026-11-18 0958c5a878b59037 Behaviour changes on purpose and the same on every platform (issue 103): the captain starts every run of the ladder in a new session and counts a run that ended at a handover as no attempt; benchmark.py refuses a walk past walks in harbour.json unless it is opened with critical=; deck_line.py takes --stowed on an accepted demo; moorings.leave removes a merged berth's containers and local branch; the mender may land a need under docker/. drive.py and check-slice-scope.py change words only
# why: 2026-11-18 5f427afbde97aeeb A further feature is drawn into the project's storyboard (#102): scripts/agents/existing.py is new and lists what the project has drawn or built; surfaces.py writes that list before the session and no longer counts a frame marked built as drawn; check-surfaces.py reads the built lines of mock-states.md to say why a slice may not name one. Each does the same on macOS and Linux

